INT. LAMPLIGHTERS' DEPOT - DAWN - 1887

Brass poles hang from iron hooks. Ladders. Oil cans. A coke stove ticking.

HOBART PRYCE, 50s, ash-grey beard, sits on an overturned crate mending a wick-trimmer. Across from him, young TESS MALLORY, 19, boots too large, sorts spent matches into a tin.

Neither speaks.

A church bell somewhere. Six strokes.

PRYCE
Poles.

Tess lifts two from the rack. Hands one over. He tests the weight. Nods.

PRYCE (CONT'D)
Gaslight's out on Fenwick Row.

TESS
Again?

PRYCE
Again.

They pull on coats. Tess drops the tin. Matches scatter across the boards. They both kneel and snatch at them, knocking heads.

TESS
Sorry.

PRYCE
Mind the sulphur.

The door bangs open. Wind. Running feet outside. Both freeze, poles half raised.

Silence.

TESS
Dogs?

PRYCE
Constables.

They glance at the window. Blue coats pass. The street goes quiet. Both exhale.

PRYCE (CONT'D)
They're after the Quigley lot.

TESS
Not us.

PRYCE
Never us. We carry fire. Nobody arrests the man with fire.

He grins. She almost does.

They step into the street. The sky is the color of dishwater. Tess lifts her pole to the first lamp. Pryce lifts his to the second. Two small flames bloom in the grey.

TESS
Mr. Pryce?

PRYCE
Aye.

TESS
What happens when they light themselves?

PRYCE
Then we find something else to carry.

They move on up the row, side by side, one lamp, then the next.
